---
import Essay from '../layouts/Essay.astro';
---

<Essay
  docNo="01"
  title="How a product gets designed"
  description="A view of product design from three seats on the same supply chain: a third-party laboratory, a system company and a global brand."
  thesis="No one designs the whole product. Every function designs a different version of the product. The customer only gets one."
>
  <div class="sub">
    <div class="sub__body">
      <p>
        At a third-party laboratory, I learned how claims are proven. At a system company, how
        products are made real. At a brand, what makes one worth building.
      </p>
      <p>
        Engineering taught me to follow the physics. The supply chain taught me where physics stops
        being the whole decision.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <section class="sub">
    <div class="sub__head">
      <span class="sub__no">1.1</span>
      <h2>Everyone is right, locally</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="sub__body">
      <p>
        Mechanical can protect the structure. Firmware can recover around it. Manufacturing can hold
        the process. A supplier can meet every drawing.
      </p>
      <div class="pullquote"><p>The product can still be wrong.</p></div>
      <p>Local correctness is not product coherence.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="sub">
    <div class="sub__head">
      <span class="sub__no">1.2</span>
      <h2>Design happens at the interfaces</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="sub__body">
      <p>
        A tolerance changes tooling. Tooling changes variation. Variation changes margin. Margin
        changes what the customer experiences.
      </p>
      <p>The hard part is seeing the chain while the product can still change.</p>
      <p>
        A supplier sees a process. The system team sees an architecture. The brand sees a promise.
        Product design makes those views agree.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="sub">
    <div class="sub__head">
      <span class="sub__no">1.3</span>
      <h2>The customer inherits the compromise</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="sub__body">
      <p>
        Customers never see the debate. They experience weight, heat, latency, durability, price,
        and whether the product still works years later.
      </p>
      <p>
        A product earns the right to exist when the compromise is coherent: it survives reality, can
        be built repeatedly, and creates enough value to justify its trade-offs.
      </p>
      <p><strong>Everything before that is a proposal.</strong></p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Essay>
